<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sutorina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sutorina is a narrow strip of coast at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-kotor">Bay of Kotor</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herceg-novi">Herceg Novi</a> in present-day Montenegro. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> of 1699 it was left as an Ottoman corridor to the sea, which, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neum">Neum</a> to the north, separated the Republic of Ragusa from Venetian territory. It later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> before being transferred to Montenegro in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sutorina.jpg" alt="Sutorina" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alenmahovic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sutorina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
